WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.798 --> 00:00:09.635
The Last Days of Ptolemy Grey
is the story of a man

00:00:09.718 --> 00:00:11.887
who lives in delusions.

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:13.514
-What's wrong with you?
-I'm so sorry.

00:00:13.597 --> 00:00:15.098
He's in his mid-90s.

00:00:15.182 --> 00:00:19.186
He's right there at the precipice
of full-out dementia.

00:00:19.269 --> 00:00:20.020
-A car--
-Hey!

00:00:21.271 --> 00:00:23.357
He's far in the back of his own mind.

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:24.733
He needs help.

00:00:24.983 --> 00:00:26.985
Seem like I can't remember
nothing no more.

00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:33.784
They go to a doctor
who has this miracle drug.

00:00:33.867 --> 00:00:36.036
After the doses are administered,

00:00:36.119 --> 00:00:39.122
Mr. Grey will remember everything
that he has ever known.

00:00:39.665 --> 00:00:43.961
The doctor has a medicine
that will bring him back to consciousness

00:00:44.044 --> 00:00:45.254
for maybe a month.

00:00:45.337 --> 00:00:46.797
This is an opportunity

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:49.800
to reclaim parts of his life
that he's been searching for.

00:00:50.342 --> 00:00:51.969
Oo! I’m goin’ home!

00:00:55.055 --> 00:00:58.225
He takes the medicine.
He regains his memory.

00:00:58.475 --> 00:01:01.228
And in a very slender window of time,

00:01:01.311 --> 00:01:06.567
he tries to do all the things
in his life that he failed at.

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:09.570
He wants to fix his relationships

00:01:09.653 --> 00:01:12.906
and solve a riddle in his mind,

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:15.117
and it drives the story.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:18.912
What is he trying to do?
And why does he need to do it so badly?

00:01:19.746 --> 00:01:20.581
Come on in.

00:01:21.331 --> 00:01:22.165
Sensia?

00:01:24.585 --> 00:01:26.503
She gone, boy.

00:01:26.587 --> 00:01:31.466
This is a man struggling
to reclaim his life through his memories.

00:01:31.884 --> 00:01:34.303
We have our present-day story,

00:01:34.386 --> 00:01:38.182
we have flashbacks to 1930s Mississippi,

00:01:38.265 --> 00:01:40.934
and we have '70s sequences.

00:01:41.018 --> 00:01:46.815
And it was really, really amazing to see
all of these things come to life.

00:01:48.233 --> 00:01:49.693
It's almost time.

00:01:49.776 --> 00:01:51.153
Best be getting home.

00:01:52.571 --> 00:01:56.575
Me and Walter been trying
to do this project for like ten years.

00:01:56.658 --> 00:01:59.203
It's a universal thing.

00:01:59.286 --> 00:02:03.582
When you have a loved one
that doesn't know who you are anymore,

00:02:03.832 --> 00:02:07.920
it's like the most heartbreaking thing
in the world.

00:02:08.879 --> 00:02:12.424
Sam has gone to a place
that he hasn't usually gone.

00:02:13.425 --> 00:02:15.761
Sam is so perfect for this role

00:02:15.844 --> 00:02:20.641
because it needs an actor
of a certain maturity

00:02:20.724 --> 00:02:23.143
and a certain genius.

00:02:23.227 --> 00:02:27.564
What's going to be really compelling
for his fans and for the audience

00:02:27.648 --> 00:02:31.985
is to see him in a role
where he is vulnerable,

00:02:32.194 --> 00:02:36.823
much older, very different
from anything we've ever seen him do.

00:02:36.907 --> 00:02:40.118
It's a fanciful story of
what someone would do

00:02:40.202 --> 00:02:44.373
if they had an opportunity to right the wrongs
of a life that they've lived.

00:02:45.165 --> 00:02:49.294
We carry such shame for the things
that we've done in our past,

00:02:49.378 --> 00:02:52.798
and at the end of the day,
you got one life to live.

00:02:52.881 --> 00:02:54.299
So what are you going to do with it
while you're here?

00:02:56.260 --> 00:02:57.845
There are times when you get it right.

00:02:58.595 --> 00:03:04.434
We had the time, and the space,
to get this right.

